{"type":"ring","title":"Two lanes and a dateline: no deadlock (4-chip ring)","intro":"A link carries data as <b>flits</b>, small fixed-size pieces of a packet (4 flits per packet here). The receiving chip keeps a buffer per link. A packet's first flit, its <b>head</b>, can enter a buffer once the previous packet's last flit has arrived there, so packets queue one behind another and their flits never mix. Here 4 chips form a ring and send clockwise, one flit per link per cycle; each link takes turns between traffic passing through and the chip's own packets. <br><br>The fix is a second <b>virtual channel</b> (VC): each link gets two lanes with separate buffers, sharing the wire. Packets start on VC 0 and move to VC 1 when they cross the <b>dateline</b>, the link from chip 3 back to chip 0. VC 0 is never used on that link, and a packet on VC 1 never reaches it again, so neither lane can close a loop. This is why the torus model uses 2 VCs, one dateline per ring. Boardfly's escape VCs use the same idea, moving up one layer per group hop.","n":4,"vcs":2,"cap":4,"plen":4,"dateline":true,"packets":[[0,1],[2,0],[1,3],[3,1],[0,2],[2,3],[0,3],[1,0],[0,3],[0,3],[1,3],[2,1],[2,1],[3,1],[1,0],[3,2],[2,0],[3,2]],"frames":[{"b":[[],[],[],[],[],[],[],[]],"q":[[[0,0]],[],[],[]],"w":[],"d":0},{"b":[[],[],[[0,0]],[],[],[],[],[]],"q":[[[0,1]],[],[[1,0]],[]],"w":[],"d":0},{"b":[[],[],[[0,1]],[],[],[],[[1,0]],[]],"q":[[[0,2]],[],[[1,1]],[]],"w":[],"d":0},{"b":[[],[[1,0]],[[0,2]],[],[],[],[[1,1]],[]],"q":[[[0,3]],[[2,0]],[[1,2]],[]],"w":[],"d":0},{"b":[[],[[1,1]],[[0,3]],[],[[2,0]],[],[[1,2]],[]],"q":[[],[[2,1]],[[1,3]],[]],"w":[[2,0,3,0]],"d":0},{"b":[[],[[1,2]],[],[],[[2,0],[2,1]],[],[[1,3]],[]],"q":[[],[[2,2]],[],[[3,0]]],"w":[],"d":1},{"b":[[],[[1,3]],[],[],[[2,1],[2,2]],[],[[2,0]],[]],"q":[[],[[2,3]],[],[[3,0]]],"w":[],"d":1},{"b":[[],[[3,0]],[],[],[[2,2],[2,3]],[],[[2,1]],[]],"q":[[],[],[],[[3,1]]],"w":[],"d":2},{"b":[[],[[3,1]],[],[[3,0]],[[2,3]],[],[[2,2]],[]],"q":[[[4,0]],[],[],